{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md | sed -n '1189,1253p'\nnl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md | sed -n '975,1050p'\"","aggregated_output":"  1189\t#### 7.7.2 Name obligations are resolved per store slice, and completion is conservative\n  1190\t\n  1191\tA name extracted from code (a `:global` row) is an **obligation**. Because this design preserves named variables across the Semantic Tuple boundary, the evaluator simply looks up the name in the environment.\n  1192\t\n  1193\t**Variables never fall through to globals.** An under-arity call leaves missing parameter names bound to `nil`; an over-arity call drops extra arguments beyond the params list length. An unbound variable name evaluates to `nil`; it never falls through to the store, primitives, or modules (since `:variable` and `:global` are distinct nodes). Therefore, name obligations are strictly and statically the set of `:global` names, never `:variable` names.\n  1194\t\n  1195\tEvery retained obligation is a required primitive or module export, checked by\n  1196\tprofile (UCF §7.5.2), and the **effects of a callable are read from its\n  1197\tprofile** (`:yin.k/effects`), never inferred from the name: `yin/def`\n  1198\tcontributes `:vm/store-put` and `require` contributes `:module/require`\n  1199\tbecause their profiles say so. Where a retained name has no profile at the\n  1200\temitter, discovery is `:incomplete`; conservatively retaining every\n  1201\tpossible primitive and module requirement is always admissible and never\n  1202\tmakes a `:complete` result wrong.\n  1203\t\n  1204\tThis rule is the amendment UCF §7.6.1's *Names* paragraph needs: its\n  1205\tsentence that a name \"is satisfied if it is bound in any environment the\n  1206\tvalue carries\" is the unsound test, and this document states the\n  1207\tcorrect rule in its place; UCF must be amended to say the same\n  1208\t(§10.8).\n  1209\t\n  1210\t#### 7.7.3 The fixed point converges over work items, not addresses\n  1211\t\n  1212\tThe unit of analysis is a **work item** `[code-address context]`, not an\n  1213\taddress. Two closures sharing one segment address with different captured\n  1214\tenvironments are two work items, because each enters the segment with its\n  1215\town environment (`semantic.cljc:199-205`) and so reaches different values (closures, streams, cursors, parked records), which\n  1216\tcontribute different work items; the name obligations of the segment are the same for both. The **context** of a work item is a finite abstraction: the\n  1217\tclosure's captured environment, and nothing else. This context\n  1218\tcontributes values (such as closures or streams bound within it), not\n  1219\tname discharge. Because values are finite, the set of contexts is finite\n  1220\tand the iteration terminates.\n  1221\t\n  1222\t- **Code once, contexts each.** Code is fetched, validated, and projected\n  1223\t  once per address; every newly discovered `[address context]` pair is\n  1224\t  analyzed, whether or not the address was seen before.\n  1225\t- **Parked ids.** Each extracted parked id must name a record in the\n  1226\t  carried `:parked` slice (UCF §7.6.3); a missing one is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`\n  1227\t  naming the id.\n  1228\t- **Values.** Closures, streams, and cursors reachable from the frame, the\n  1229\t  K frames, parked records, the store slice, and the pending wait\n  1230\t  contribute work items and stream identities as §7.6.1 specifies; a\n  1231\t  closure contributes `[its segment address, its captured-env context]`.\n  1232\t- **Modules.** A required module with an undeclared footprint sets\n  1233\t  `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete`, as §7.6.1 already rules.\n  1234\t\n  1235\t**Convergence** is reached when one full pass adds nothing to any of:\n  1236\tthe work-item set, the obligation set, the discovered\n  1237\tvalue set (closures, streams, cursors, parked records), the store-key\n  1238\trequirement, the FFI-op requirement, the effect set after normalization\n  1239\t(§7.7.1), and the callable and module footprints. A pass that adds a new\n  1240\tcontext, a new profile fact, or a new footprint continues the iteration\n  1241\teven when no new address appeared.\n  1242\t\n  1243\tDiscovery is `:complete` only when every reachable work item was\n  1244\tanalyzed, every `:global` obligation was discharged by the store slice by key or retained\n  1245\tas a profiled primitive or module requirement, every parked id resolved, and every module\n  1246\tfootprint was declared. It is `:blocked` when an address cannot be\n  1247\tfetched and `:incomplete` otherwise. Recasting the walk as Datalog changes\n  1248\twhere the facts come from, not the conservatism of the closure.\n  1249\t\n  1250\tUCF §7.6.1 has the same defect in its own terms: \"repeats until nothing\n  1251\tnew is reachable\" is stated over segments and values, and its walk of \"a\n  1252\tnewly fetched segment\" is per address. It needs this same amendment\n  1253\t(§10.8).\n   975\tplus the structural rules a segment needs, nothing else. Both checkers\n   976\tmirror `code/well-formed?` (`code.cljc:155-180`): each returns nil or the\n   977\tfirst defect, rules run in order, and each rule may assume the earlier ones\n   978\theld.\n   979\t\n   980\t### 7.4 Row rules\n   981\t\n   982\tDefects name a path from the root row (§2.5), or the row's own id for a\n   983\trow nothing reaches.\n   984\t\n   985\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   986\t| Rule              | Defect when                                                                                                                                       |\n   987\t+===================+===================================================================================================================================================+\n   988\t| `:tag`            | the element after the id is not a tag of §2.3                                                                                                     |\n   989\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   990\t| `:arity`          | the count differs from the tag's body arity plus one                                                                                              |\n   991\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   992\t| `:slot-kind`      | a slot's value is not of the slot's kind: a `node` slot that is not an address, a `nodes` slot that is not a vector of addresses, a `data` or     |\n   993\t|                   | `key` slot failing `plain-data?`, a `bool` slot that is not `true`/`false`                                                                        |\n   994\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   995\t| `:saturation`     | a saturated slot (§2.4) is nil                                                                                                                    |\n   996\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   997\t| `:id-resolves`    | a child id in a `node` or `nodes` slot names no row of the loaded set                                                                             |\n   998\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   999\t| `:acyclic`        | following child ids from a row revisits a row already on its own path                                                                             |\n  1000\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1001\t| `:root-reachable` | a row of the loaded set is not reachable from the root row                                                                                        |\n  1002\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1003\t| `:variable-bounds`| a `:variable` name must be bound by an enclosing `:lambda`'s params, else the frontend must have emitted `:global`; the defect names the path     |\n  1004\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1005\t\n  1006\tThe three reference rules are the row counterpart of §7.5's\n  1007\t`:target-bounds`: a row's child slots are references, and they must\n  1008\tresolve within the loaded set and close under the root, as a jump target\n  1009\tmust land inside its segment. Acyclicity is also implied by the per-row\n  1010\taddress check whenever it runs — a row's id commits to its children's\n  1011\tids, so a cycle would break the hash (§4.1) — and the rule stands\n  1012\tregardless, so that a load taking ids as given still names the defect.\n  1013\t\n  1014\tThere is no encoder-domain rule: §4.2 blocks identity uses outright rather\n  1015\tthan admitting a domain.\n  1016\t\n  1017\t### 7.5 Vector rules\n  1018\t\n  1019\tDefects name a pc. Rules 1–4 of `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.6 (one segment,\n  1020\tdense pcs, sorted by pc, refs resolve to entities) are satisfied by the\n  1021\tvector form by construction and have no rule here; rules 5 and 6 and the\n  1022\tUCF §7.3.4 checks are translated as follows.\n  1023\t\n  1024\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1025\t| Rule             | Defect when                                                                                                                                         |\n  1026\t+==================+=====================================================================================================================================================+\n  1027\t| `:nonempty`      | the vector has no instructions                                                                                                                      |\n  1028\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1029\t| `:mnemonic`      | the first element is not a mnemonic of `code/mnemonics` (`code.cljc:12-16`)                                                                         |\n  1030\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1031\t| `:arity`         | the tuple's count differs from the mnemonic's arity in `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.4 as saturated by UCF §7.3.2                                         |\n  1032\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1033\t| `:operand-kind`  | an operand is not of its kind: `:const` and `:store-put` values or `:store-get`/`:store-put` keys fail `plain-data?` (the `key` kind of §2.2), a    |\n  1034\t|                 